  "title": { "en": "Family Scenarios", "so": "Xaaladaha Qoyska" },
  "swTitle": "Familia",
  "description": {
    "en": "Name family members, talk about your family, and handle a visit to a Kenyan home.",
    "so": "Magacaw xubnaha qoyska, ka hadal qoyskaaga, oo si fiican u maaree booqasho guri Kenya ah."
  },
  "goals": ["family"],
  "guide": [
    {
      "title": { "en": "My, your: yangu, yako", "so": "Kayga, kaaga: yangu, yako" },
      "body": {
        "en": "Most family words take yangu (my) and yako (your): mama yangu (my mother), kaka yako (your brother). For more than one person, use wangu: wazazi wangu (my parents).",
        "so": "Erayada qoyska badankood waxay qaataan yangu (kayga/tayda) iyo yako (kaaga/taada): mama yangu (hooyaday), kaka yako (walaalkaa). Wax ka badan hal qof, isticmaal wangu: wazazi wangu (waalidkay)."
      },
      "examples": [
        { "sw": "Huyu ni mama yangu.", "en": "This is my mother.", "so": "Tani waa hooyaday.", "v": "standard" },
        { "sw": "wazazi wangu", "en": "my parents", "so": "waalidkay", "v": "standard" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Kenyan family words", "so": "Erayada qoyska ee Kenya" },
      "body": {
        "en": "In Kenya nyanya usually means grandmother, and bibi often means wife (in Tanzania bibi is grandmother). Mzee wangu, 'my old man', is a common way to say 'my dad'. Kenyans also use English words like auntie and uncle.",
        "so": "Kenya, nyanya badanaa waxay ka dhigan tahay ayeeyo, bibina badanaa waxay ka dhigan tahay xaas (Tanzania bibi waa ayeeyo). Mzee wangu, 'odaygayga', waa qaab caadi ah oo lagu yiraahdo 'aabahay'. Kenyaanku sidoo kale waxay isticmaalaan erayo Ingiriis ah sida auntie iyo uncle."
      },
      "examples": [
        { "sw": "nyanya", "en": "grandmother", "so": "ayeeyo", "v": "everyday" },
        { "sw": "mzee wangu", "en": "my dad (casual)", "so": "aabahay (si fudud)", "v": "everyday" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Visiting a Kenyan home", "so": "Booqashada guri Kenya ah" },
      "body": {
        "en": "Guests hear Karibu! and Kaa chini (Sit down). Tea is almost always offered. To stop eating politely, say Asante, nimeshiba (Thank you, I'm full). Asking directly, like Nipe maji (Give me water), is normal and not rude in Kenya.",
        "so": "Martidu waxay maqlaan Karibu! iyo Kaa chini (Fadhiiso). Shaah had iyo jeer waa la soo bandhigaa. Si edeb leh u joojinta cunnada, dheh Asante, nimeshiba (Mahadsanid, waan dhergay). Si toos ah wax u weydiisashadu, sida Nipe maji (Biyo i sii), waa caadi Kenya, ma aha edeb darro."
      },
      "examples": [
        { "sw": "Kaa chini.", "en": "Sit down.", "so": "Fadhiiso.", "v": "everyday" },
        { "sw": "Asante, nimeshiba.", "en": "Thank you, I'm full.", "so": "Mahadsanid, waan dhergay.", "v": "standard" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Married: two different verbs", "so": "Guursaday: laba fal oo kala duwan" },
      "body": {
        "en": "Swahili uses different verbs for men and women. A man marries: ameoa (he is married). A woman is married: ameolewa (she is married).",
        "so": "Sawaaxiligu wuxuu ragga iyo dumarka u isticmaalaa falal kala duwan. Nin ayaa guursada: ameoa (wuu guursaday). Naag ayaa la guursadaa: ameolewa (way guursatay)."
      }
    }
  ],
